# Copy to .env.local. Nothing in here is a secret in production — see the note
# on SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY below — but .env.local is gitignored all the same.
#
# For local development the values come from `supabase status`.

# The Supabase project's API origin. Locally this is the CLI's stack; in
# production it is https://<project-ref>.supabase.co.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321

# Safe in the browser, and it has to be: every page the app renders reads
# through it. Row-level security is what protects the data, not this key.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...

# Bypasses RLS entirely. **Read by the test suites only** — `lib/db/test-support.ts`
# and `e2e/support.ts`, both of which need to see what an anonymous submission
# actually stored, which no anonymous client is allowed to read back. No
# application code reads it, so it does not belong in the deployment's
# environment: the deployed app runs on the three NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables and
# holds no secret at all. Keep it that way.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_...

# The app's own public origin, without a trailing slash. Magic-link emails
# point back here, so it is set explicitly rather than derived from the Host
# header — a spoofed host would otherwise decide where a sign-in link sends
# somebody. It must match (or be listed in) the Supabase project's Site URL and
# redirect allow-list: locally that is supabase/config.toml's auth.site_url /
# additional_redirect_urls, in production the Authentication → URL
# Configuration screen. See docs/DEPLOY.md.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000
